## Your worktree

You work in `{{ tree }}`, your own branch of the checkout `{{ repo }}`. Edit, build, test and commit there and nowhere else; do not touch `{{ repo }}`. Read paths relative to your worktree where the prompt names the checkout. Commit with

    git -C {{ tree }} add <path> [path...] && git -C {{ tree }} commit -s -m <message>

Run a check on your work with `--tree {{ tree }}`. When you finish, the orchestrator audits your commits and rebases them onto the checkout; uncommitted changes are not integrated and fail the task.
{% if behind %}
## Your branch is behind the checkout

{{ behind }} commit(s) landed on the checkout after your branch was cut; its head is `{{ head }}`. Rebase onto that head before anything else, for this turn only despite the rule against it:

    git -C {{ tree }} rebase {{ head }}

Resolve each conflict so that both your change and the landed one stand, then `git -C {{ tree }} add` the resolved files and `GIT_EDITOR=true git -C {{ tree }} rebase --continue` until the rebase is done. If the two cannot both stand, `git -C {{ tree }} rebase --abort` and say why in your decision file.
{% endif %}
